<p>Anthropic and Accenture announced a new model-evaluation partnership on September 18, 2026. Each company says it expects to invest at least $1 billion over five years, creating at least $2 billion in combined capacity for independent evaluation work. The unusual part is not only the size. Anthropic says evaluators will work inside the company with access comparable to an employee.</p>
<figure class="article-image"><img src="/assets/images/blog/anthropic-accenture-embedded-ai-evaluation-2026.svg" width="1200" height="630" alt="Embedded AI evaluation workflow showing independent evaluators inside a frontier AI lab"></figure>
<h2>What Anthropic and Accenture actually announced</h2><p>The partnership will be led by Faculty, Accenture’s specialist AI business. Anthropic says the work will include model evaluation, red-teaming, alignment assessments and testing of safeguards.</p><p>Anthropic says the goal is to let evaluators observe models and the decisions around training and deployment from inside the company. That could make it easier to identify blind spots that are hard to see from outside.</p>
<h2>Why embedded evaluation is different</h2><p>Traditional external evaluation usually starts with a model or product that is already available to the evaluator. Embedded evaluation changes the vantage point. Evaluators can see how systems are developed, what safety decisions were made and how incidents are handled.</p><p>That access can improve evidence, but it also creates a trust question. An evaluator working inside a company needs clear independence, reporting rights and protection from pressure if the findings are uncomfortable.</p>
<h2>Anthropic says the standards are not settled</h2><p>This is important. Anthropic explicitly says there are not yet common standards for what embedded evaluators should access or how they should report findings. It also says there is no settled long-term funding system for independent evaluation.</p><p>So this announcement should be treated as an early operating model, not a finished global standard.</p>
<h2>What a strong evaluation program should measure</h2><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Useful evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Model behavior</td><td>Repeatable tests, failure rates and changes across versions</td></tr><tr><td>Agent safety</td><td>Tool use, escalation, permissions and external side effects</td></tr><tr><td>Safeguards</td><td>Whether controls work under realistic pressure and adversarial inputs</td></tr><tr><td>Incident handling</td><td>Detection time, investigation quality and remediation evidence</td></tr><tr><td>Independence</td><td>Evaluator access, reporting rights and conflict controls</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>What enterprise AI teams can copy</h2><p>Most companies will not spend billions on evaluation. They can still copy the structure. Give an independent reviewer access to representative workflows, require written test cases, keep evidence of failures, and retest after fixes.</p><p>For agent systems, test more than the model's answer. Include credentials, plugins, APIs, files, approvals and the final action. The <a href="/claude/anthropic-september-2026-threat-report">Anthropic threat reporting</a> and the recent <a href="/tools-guide/ai-agent-software-testing-workflow">AI-agent software testing workflow</a> are useful companion reads for this approach.</p>
<h2>What the $2 billion figure does not mean</h2><p>The combined commitment does not mean $2 billion is being spent immediately or that every dollar is already assigned to specific evaluations. Anthropic says each company expects to invest at least $1 billion over five years in building capacity.</p><p>It also does not guarantee that evaluations will find every dangerous behavior. The value depends on access, test quality, independence and whether findings lead to real changes.</p>
<h2>Questions to ask an AI vendor</h2><ul><li>Who can independently test the model?</li><li>What access do evaluators receive?</li><li>Can evaluators publish or report serious findings?</li><li>Are safety tests repeated after major model changes?</li><li>Are real tool-use and agent workflows included?</li></ul>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>How much are Anthropic and Accenture investing?</h3><p>Each says it expects to invest at least $1 billion over five years, for at least $2 billion combined.</p><h3>Will evaluators work inside Anthropic?</h3><p>Yes. Anthropic describes the model as embedded evaluation, with access comparable to an employee.</p><h3>Is there already a global standard for embedded evaluation?</h3><p>No. Anthropic says standards for access, reporting and long-term funding are still being developed.</p>
